WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:09.840
BARBARA HUGHEY: OK.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.620
So as Kristen said,
I'm Barbara Hughey.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.266
I teach in the Mechanical
Engineering Department at MIT.

00:00:15.266 --> 00:00:17.890
And I thought you might want to
just see a little bit about me.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.435
So I was more or
less from New Jersey.

00:00:20.435 --> 00:00:23.714
I was born in Philadelphia,
lived in New Jersey and outside

00:00:23.714 --> 00:00:25.620
of Philadelphia.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.790
There we go.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.820
I moved back and forth about
every two and a half years.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.415
Briefly moved to Southern
California when I moved.

00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:32.729
And in fact I was an
undergraduate at Princeton

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.690
where I went in as
a physics major.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.190
I had always loved
math and physics.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.900
I ended up coming out with
an engineering physics

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.530
degree in the
mechanical and aerospace

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.826
engineering department, which
was awesome for what I'm

00:00:42.826 --> 00:00:44.700
doing now, and also for
a lot of the research

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.610
I did before I came back to MIT.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.460
And then I came up to Boston to
get my PhD in physics at MIT.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.110
Very highly esoteric
subject that

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.080
was great fun involving
quantum mechanics,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.464
but not terribly useful.

00:00:57.464 --> 00:00:59.380
And then I worked for a
couple small companies

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.610
in the area mostly doing
applications of particle

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.816
accelerators to medicine.

00:01:03.816 --> 00:01:05.190
And actually a
lot of it-- I know

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.356
you're spending the day on
sort of imaging and image

00:01:07.356 --> 00:01:09.820
processing-- I did a lot with
positron emission Tomography

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.410
images, magnetic MRI
images, things like that.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.960
And then in 2002
I came back to MIT

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.520
to teach the undergraduate
measurement and instrumentation

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.890
class, where I basically teach
students how to do experiments.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.197
And we have a project we
call Go Forth and Measure,

00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:26.780
where each student picks their
own project and does something.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.610
Actually, Kristen, what was
your Go Forth and Measure?

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.041
What was your go forth
and measure project?

00:01:34.041 --> 00:01:34.540
KRISTEN: Me?

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:34.952
BARBARA HUGHEY: You.

00:01:34.952 --> 00:01:35.452
KRISTEN: Oh.

00:01:35.452 --> 00:01:38.260
So does anyone like to run here?

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:38.760
Track?

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.680
Awesome.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.940
Me too.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.722
I took a pressure sensor
and put it inside a sneaker,

00:01:44.722 --> 00:01:46.430
and I tried running
on different surfaces

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.980
and compared the
impact of running

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.675
on a treadmill versus
running on concrete.

00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:54.050
BARBARA HUGHEY: And we have
a lot of really cool projects

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.880
like that where people do
everyday things that they're

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.170
interested in, including
food, things like that.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.290
So anyways, back to the
women's technology program.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.520
So I came to MIT in 2002.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.580
In 2006 I was asked to expand
the existing women's technology

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.710
program which was founded in
2002 in electrical engineering

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:12.740
and computer science.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.840
So most of what you're doing
today would fit more in that.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.630
And then we expanded it
to mechanical engineering.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.990
And the goal is to spark
high school girl's interest

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.280
in studying engineering
and computer science, which

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.810
is I think exactly the
goal of this program, too.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.070
So our approach is to pick
girls to pick students

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.120
who are rising high
school seniors who

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.300
are really good and really
interested in math and science,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.846
but don't really have a
background in engineering.

00:02:37.846 --> 00:02:39.720
Have maybe heard of
engineering, people maybe

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.230
tell them you should
think about engineering,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:42.940
but they don't really
know what it is.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.940
They haven't taken a lot
of engineering classes.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.450
They're just
curious and not sure

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.100
what they want to major in.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.700
So we give them a four week
exposure to engineering.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.170
There are two curriculum
tracks, electrical engineering

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.253
and computer science, and
then the one that I run,

00:02:55.253 --> 00:02:56.847
which is mechanical engineering.

00:02:56.847 --> 00:02:59.430
I don't have a lot of time so I
can't go into too much detail,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:00.971
but I'll be happy
to answer questions

00:03:00.971 --> 00:03:02.560
about what we do in them.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.197
They're mostly hands on classes,
like what you're doing today.

00:03:05.197 --> 00:03:07.280
We of course-- especially
mechanical engineering--

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.770
have to teach them
a lot of physics,

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.894
because you can't do much
in mechanical engineering

00:03:10.894 --> 00:03:12.190
until you have physics.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.220
But physics is fun.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.300
It really is.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.360
And then try to correct ideas
about what engineers are like

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.220
and what they do.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.450
And the students give
us wonderful quotes,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.060
such as that they
realize engineering

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.960
has so much more depth and
so many more applications.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.600
Coming in they have some of the
preconceptions that you might

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.570
have, which is that
engineers work alone,

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.090
it's not very exciting,
not very people oriented.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.040
But in fact it is.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.870
You work a lot in teams, as
I think you're doing today.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.480
And you get to do a whole
range of applications

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.210
and make products that
are helpful to people.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.410
We also want to increase
confidence of the students

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.289
by exposing them to female
engineers of all ages.

00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:50.830
Give them an
opportunity to live away

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.730
from home-- so it's four
weeks living away from home.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.340
The students live
in the MIT dorms.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.130
They live with MIT undergrads
are recent graduates who

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.530
help them with their
homework, and also do

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.810
social outings and everything.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.380
And the classes are taught
by graduate students

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.060
in mechanical
engineering or EECS.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.330
And-- I already said
some of this-- so again,

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.400
it's a hands on.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.590
There's 40 students
in the EECS track

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.290
and 20 students in the
mechanical engineering track,

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.529
so there's 60 students total.

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:22.747
We have hands on lab
classes with projects

00:04:22.747 --> 00:04:23.830
in mechanical engineering.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.010
We like to have a sort
of capstone project

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.470
at the end of each week,
which ranges from building

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.910
cranes that can sustain a
certain amount of weight,

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.620
through doing oral
presentations,

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.470
making a poster presentation.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.060
And then there are two special
final week presentations

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.400
where the EECS students
build DC motors,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.160
and the WTP-ME students
build Rube Goldberg machines,

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.650
which are basically just
chain reaction machines.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.444
But the thing we do
to make it possibly

00:04:49.444 --> 00:04:51.610
a little more interesting
is they have to figure out

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:52.926
what's going on in every step.

00:04:52.926 --> 00:04:54.300
So it's not just
trial and error,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.820
they have to apply the design
principles we've taught them.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.290
They have to use the
physics and the science

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.340
we've taught them to
figure out what's going on,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.220
and then make their machine run.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.510
And we also do a lot of faculty
and industry guest speakers

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:06.950
and tours.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.380
We also have, as I said, MIT
students and recent graduates

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.060
are the instructors
and also the tutors

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:15.670
that live with the students.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.340
And these are some
photos from the motor

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.620
building for the EECS.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:21.200
So this is actually the
professor who runs it.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.960
They figure out the
design of their motor,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.190
then they do all the machining.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.231
They build them in pairs.

00:05:25.231 --> 00:05:27.190
So that's one of the
completed motors.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.550
And they use actually the
mechanical engineering shop

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.520
to do all this.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.790
And then here's some
photographs from ME

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.890
the track where you see
a Rube Goldberg machine.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.820
Dominoes are of
course often used.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.050
Various types of trip
wires and things.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.171
Usually pendulums
and Slinkys used.

00:05:45.171 --> 00:05:46.420
And then there's a final step.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.020
They can decide.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.280
This one for their
final step, they

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.446
pulled aside a curtain that
then showed a photograph

00:05:50.446 --> 00:05:54.600
of all the students and staff.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.120
So since 2002-- reminder
again, ME started in 2006,

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.220
but EECS started in 2002.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:07.110
Up through last summer we've had
744 females finish the program.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:11.149
They all have a-- they say
WTP has a big impact on them.

00:06:11.149 --> 00:06:13.690
Many of them-- as I'll share on
the next slide-- many of them

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.660
go into engineering.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.209
Some of them after
going through WTP

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:19.250
decide that they actually
don't like engineering.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.030
It's not for them.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:21.680
That's a perfectly
great outcome for us

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.099
as well, because then they
have learned and saved

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:26.640
their parents money of sending
them to an engineering school.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.340
They've learned, oh I don't
really like engineering.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.560
I want to do something else.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.070
So either outcome is fine.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.210
We just want to expose
them and give them

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.190
a real more realistic view of
what engineering actually is.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:39.720
Many of them would not have
thought of an engineering major

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.860
or applying to MIT.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.260
And as you see,
actually many of them

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.670
end up majoring in either
engineering or computer

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:47.170
science.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:50.100
More than 60% of the
alumni have majored

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.720
or are majoring in engineering
or computer science.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.525
Many others in
science, math, there's

00:06:55.525 --> 00:06:59.570
a big bunch in business,
and then a few of others.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:02.210
So it's highly successful
for its goal, which really

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:05.830
is to get girls interested
in majoring in engineering,

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.710
or at the very least to let them
know what engineering is about.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:13.440
And this is, I think, the
class of 2014, probably.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.280
And that's our website.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.140
And so if you have
any questions--

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.189
no-- acknowledgements first.

00:07:19.189 --> 00:07:20.230
I couldn't do this alone.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.040
There's a woman
who runs the EECS

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.120
program and the whole program.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.489
The ME program is funded
by an amazing woman who

00:07:26.489 --> 00:07:28.030
is in her-- actually
I think she just

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.370
turned 80, who is a
mechanical engineer.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.150
And she funds our program.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.950
And then various other people.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.200
So, any questions?

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.720
Nothing.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.080
OK.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.229
OK, and if you are not-- I know
there are some juniors here,

00:07:46.229 --> 00:07:48.770
which unfortunately have missed
the deadline for this summer.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.840
But if you are not a junior,
if you're an eighth, ninth,

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.870
or 10th grader, look
in around November

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:57.700
of your junior year on
that website right there

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.462
and look for
application information.

00:07:59.462 --> 00:08:01.670
And the applications are
usually due at the beginning

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:02.820
of January.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.020
OK?

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.720
OK.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.270
[APPLAUSE]

